      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we first need to find her walking rate in miles per minute. We know that Jana takes 24 minutes to walk one mile. Therefore, her rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nThis means that Jana walks 1 mile every 24 minutes. To find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes, we set up a proportion based on her walking rate:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} = \\frac{x \\text{ miles}}{10 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we cross-multiply:\n\n\\[\n1 \\text{ mile} \\times 10 \\text{ minutes} = x \\text{ miles} \\times 24 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n10 = 24x\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10}{24}\n\\]\n\nTo express \\(x\\) as a decimal to the nearest tenth, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} = 0.416666\\ldots\n\\]\n\nRounding 0.416666... to the nearest tenth gives us:\n\n\\[\n0.4\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
